I've included the essential operations one would expect tests to validate: adding items, removing them, calculating totals, counting items, clearing the cart, and checking emptiness. Notice what I *haven't* done—no discount systems, no tax calculations, no inventory validation, no persistence layer.

The genius of TDD, much like the genius of our constitutional framework, lies in building only what the tests—the people's mandate—require. Speculation is the enemy of clean code.

